17592186217799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217244 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Wronskian Differential Equation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217244 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186217003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217244 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "357839bc-6cf7-532d-a4cd-ce4b64aefc41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217245 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186217800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186217803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217245 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Wronskian Using Abel's Identity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217245 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186217004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217245 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "84813773-2fbc-5752-8f31-8e547c9f79e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217246 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186217801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217246 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186217802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217246 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217246 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217246 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217246 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217246 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217246 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217246 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217246 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217246 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217246 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217246 #edb/ref 1039 "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217246 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186217005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217246 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217326 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186218195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217326 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186218196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217326 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186218197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217326 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186218198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217326 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186218199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217326 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186218200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217326 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217326 #edb/ref 1045 "Abel's Identity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217326 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186218195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217326 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217326 #edb/ref 1050 780.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217326 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0db9329c-dec7-5a3e-a676-325cf54ff03f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217802 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217802 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217802 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217802 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342724" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217802 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217802 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(0)=1.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(0)=1,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217802 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ac49bd9-9719-507d-b361-709e747ff5e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217802 #edb/ref 1074 "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=2W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =2W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =2\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =2x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{2x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{2\\cdot 0} \\\\ 1 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)={e}^{2x}$ and $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218195 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186218196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218195 #edb/ref 1061 600667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218195 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e7527729-b857-5e9b-bea8-a7cbd99e635d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218195 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186218358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218196 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186218197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218196 #edb/ref 1061 600668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218196 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bb9b873b-720d-52a3-af19-21b0736163e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218196 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186217244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218197 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186218200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218197 #edb/ref 1061 600671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218197 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "21049594-a386-58b7-948b-933fb27c4926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218197 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186217245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218198 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186218199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218198 #edb/ref 1061 600674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218198 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1a5dec47-1b73-5c78-99d1-b02f62ec0319" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218198 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186217246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218199 #edb/ref 1061 600678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218199 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "888a5fbb-9e7e-52b5-8de1-71d906dc8972" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218199 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186218359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218200 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186218198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218200 #edb/ref 1061 600679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218200 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f86b886d-8ad5-5177-85d6-59b75ecbb221" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218200 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186218360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218358 #edb/ref 1064 "The Wronskian is a quick way to check whether two solutions of a homogeneous ODE are linearly independent.\n\nIn this lesson, we learn an identity, which tells us how the Wronskian changes with $x$ for an equation of the form \n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the second-order linear homogeneous ODE above on some interval $I.$ Let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian:\n![](images/bf/bf982a342681e4142c476f52c77cb95836bc024d75a0c62961fc5f0d6d71320b.svg)\n\nThen, **Abel's identity** states that the Wronskian satisfies the first-order linear differential equation\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nSolving this equation via separation of variables gives the general form for $W(x){:}$\n\n$$\nW(x) = C e^{-\\int P(x) \\, \\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nThis allows us to determine the behavior of the Wronskian without knowing the solutions $y_1$ and $y_2.$\n\nFor example, consider the second-order homogeneous differential equation \n\n$$\ny''+3xy'+e^x y=0\n$$\n\nwith solutions $y_1$ and $y_2,$ and the corresponding Wronskian $W(x).$ Let's determine the differential equation that $W(x)$ must satisfy.\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & =-3xW(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** If the equation is not already in standard form, we should rewrite it before applying Abel’s identity." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218358 #edb/ref 1065 14746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218358 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "df1133ef-ff8b-5e3c-bbce-c0f1a484db10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218358 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218359 #edb/ref 1064 "*Abel's identity* says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+p(x)y'+q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their *Wronskian* $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-p(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nTo prove it, we start with the definition\n\n$$\nW(x)=y_1y_2'-y_2y_1'\n$$\n\nand differentiate both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\cancel{{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}}+{y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{″}-\\cancel{{y}_{2}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{″} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{″}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of $y''+p(x)y'+q(x)y=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny_1''=-p(x)y_1'-q(x)y_1\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny_2''=-p(x)y_2'-q(x)y_2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into the expression for $W'(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{″}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{″} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(-p(x){y}_{2}^{′}-q(x){y}_{2})-{y}_{2}(-p(x){y}_{1}^{′}-q(x){y}_{1}) \\\\  & =-p(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-\\cancel{q(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}}+p(x){y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}+\\cancel{q(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}} \\\\  & =-p(x)({y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}) \\\\  & =-p(x)W(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $W'(x)=-p(x)W(x)$, as required." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218359 #edb/ref 1065 14753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218359 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e1263afb-6125-5c5f-8c88-5025fd9457db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218359 #edb/ref 1067 "The Proof of Abel's Identity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218360 #edb/ref 1064 "Because the exponential factor in Abel's identity is *never zero*, we can deduce the following facts:\n\n- If $W(x_0)=0$ at some point $x_0 \\in I,$ then $W(x) = 0$ for all $x \\in I.$\n\n- If $W(x_0)\\neq 0,$ then $W(x) \\neq 0$ for all $x \\in I.$\n\nLet's see how this works on concrete examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218360 #edb/ref 1065 14754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218360 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1ff5ce47-4514-52a4-9f42-103d6b73b928" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218360 #edb/ref 1067 "Applications of Abel's Identity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218631517