ef 17592186315187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316751 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316751 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316751 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186316329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316751 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316751 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316751 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186316329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316751 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "58339bb5-e525-5e23-8c9a-df4ecfa1365d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316961 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316961 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316961 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316961 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316961 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316961 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186315282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316961 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b15c37ad-1987-5287-8c8f-8494de399fe3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317090 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186316240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317090 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317090 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317090 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186316240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317090 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e40c8ae7-36d2-5187-91d9-5e9873b26864" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317382 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317382 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343279" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317382 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317382 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+(\\frac{2}{x}+1){y}^{′}+(2-x)y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=3.$ What is $W(2)?$\n\n$W(2)=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317382 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c8b7de5b-aac2-510e-98ee-6d02baa0c52b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317382 #edb/ref 1074 "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2}{x}+1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-(\\frac{2}{x}+1)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-(\\frac{2}{x}+1)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-(\\frac{2}{x}+1)\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -(\\frac{2}{x}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{x}^{-2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=3$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =\\frac{C}{{1}^{2}}{e}^{-1} \\\\ 3 & =C{e}^{-1} \\\\ C & =3e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=\\frac{3e}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =\\frac{3e}{{2}^{2}}{e}^{-2}=\\frac{3}{4e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397617 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397617 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397617 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397617 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397617 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397617 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(0)=-1.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(0)=-1,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397617 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "65f6d9f7-7688-5460-b686-2b9904fe578a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397617 #edb/ref 1074 "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=4W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =4W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =4\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =4x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{4x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=-1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{4\\cdot 0} \\\\ -1 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=-{e}^{4x}$ and $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397618 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397618 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397618 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397618 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397618 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397618 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186397619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397618 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "622cd65e-d7fb-587b-8c10-1defb970706b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397619 #edb/ref 1080 